 to end basic coven 19 protocols  like testing and mandatory isolation for  positive cases this comes after two  weeks of strong opposition from doctors  in the province but the chief medical  officer of health says she's simply  following the latest science tom vernon  joins us now from edmonton tom what is  the new evidence that led to this change  robin dr dina hinshaw says  hospitalizations in alberta are trending  higher than they had originally forecast  they're also keeping a close eye on  rising pediatric cases south of the  border this coming monday alberta was  due to end mandatory isolation four  positive cases of covet 19. testing at  assessment centers for symptomatic  albertans was going to be wound down  they're going to end the mass mandate  for public transit taxes and ride shares  now these measures will now remain in  place for another six weeks until  september 27th the province stopped  notifying close contacts of positive  cases two weeks ago that measure will  not return now this comes after two  weeks of intense protests against the  government and strong opposition from  doctors teachers and parents among  others premier kenny has been the main  target of that anger he was noticeably  absent from today's announcement the ndp  says premier kenny is hiding from the  issue political watchers agree this  definitely isn't a good look so if he  wants to look like he's a leader which  seems to be  his desire  then  he should be taking responsibility for  his good decisions as well as the ones  that need to be modified or changed  robin the province also announced its  back-to-school plan today there will be  no province-wide mandate on masking  social distancing or co-harding those  decisions will be left up to local  school boards positive cases inside the  classroom as well as students showing  core symptoms of covet-19 will still  need to isolate robin tom vernon in  edmonton thanks tom  still ahead the fears for thousands of  british colombians as explosive  wildfires rage during another heat wave  to the state of emergency in bc where a  third summer heat wave is adding fuel to  fierce wildfires that are exploding  this is my life's work this has been my  home for 72 years  overnight 2 000 residents in logan lake  60 kilometers from kamloops fled their  homes after the tremont creek wildfire  jumped containment lines and closed in  it's one of 268 wildfires burning across  the province 51 fires started this week  12 in the last two days  it's been such a threatening emotional  time to deal with they were trying to  back burn from the road towards the fire  so it didn't cross the road obviously uh  fire is unpredictable and it will  definitely choose its own path so we've  been proactive and i think it helps but  um  there's  a lot of anxiety around being evacuated  at least 55 evacuation orders are in  effect across bc tonight there is little  relief in sight with the extreme heat  and gusty winds that will continue until  at least sunday  the province's forestry department fears  some of the fires will burn until the  snow falls  finding a home for retired whales up  next nova scotia's plan for an ocean  sanctuary  there's an ambitious effort underway to  create north america's first retirement  home for whales from marine parks the  sanctuary will be located in nova scotia  but as ross lord explains the project  faces uncharted waters there's only two  and a half years finding a better home  for captive whales is easier said than  done but a group that's been working for  five years on a whale sanctuary says  it's achieving new milestones after  considering hundreds of locations and  selecting one near sherbrooke nova  scotia organizers have established the  boundaries for a whale retirement home  the size of 84 football fields we now  have uh delineated identified  the actual  spot where the sanctuary is going to be  the sanctuary would be the first of its  kind in north america housing about a  dozen